(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-i-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies I Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has 3 weeks of fatigue, pallor, bruising with petechiae (pinpoint skin bleeds), bone pain and fever. He has hepatosplenomegaly. White blood cell count is 48,000 per microliter (normal 5,000-15,000) with many lymphoid blasts, hemoglobin 7.9 g/dL (normal 11.0-14.0 g/dL) and platelets 31,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Infectious mononucleosis","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Acute myeloid leukemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute lymphoblastic leukemia is the most common malignancy under age 15. He shows anemia (fatigue, pallor), thrombocytopenia (petechiae), bone pain from excessive marrow production, organomegaly and a white count above 30,000 with lymphoid blasts. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 22, 14, 24, 31"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has bone pain, anemia, kidney dysfunction, hypercalcemia and repeated infections. His chemistry panel shows an elevated protein level from excess production of a single monoclonal antibody. Which hematologic malignancy is most likely?","choices":["Multiple myeloma","Hodgkin lymphoma","Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Multiple myeloma arises from plasma cells, the antibody producers, and is marked by excess production of a single monoclonal antibody (the M protein). Bone pain, anemia, renal dysfunction and hypercalcemia (the CRAB (calcium, renal, anemia, bone) features) with infections from immune compromise fit it. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 11, 16"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia in an urgent care clinic. Which plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Admit and refer to a leukemia specialist","Outpatient blood counts in one month","Refer to a hematologist next quarter","Start oral iron and recheck"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute myeloid leukemia requires referral to and management by a hematologist/oncologist, ideally a leukemia specialist, and most patients with acute leukemia are admitted initially for work-up and initiation of treatment. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has Philadelphia chromosome-negative acute lymphoblastic leukemia (no 9;22 translocation) and is starting treatment. How should her chemotherapy plan differ from a 25-year-old's?","choices":["Arsenic trioxide with retinoic acid","Changes and dose adjustments for tolerance","The same pediatric-like regimen unchanged","A tyrosine kinase inhibitor in place of it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pediatric-like intensive regimens are usually given below age 40; in older adults like her, therapy changes and dose adjustments may be necessary for tolerance. Her disease is Philadelphia-negative, so no tyrosine kinase inhibitor is indicated. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has polycythemia vera with a hematocrit of 58% (normal 41-50%), headaches and blurry vision. Which treatment lowers his red cell excess directly?","choices":["Leukapheresis","Red cell transfusion","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent","Phlebotomy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of polycythemia vera is phlebotomy, with hydroxyurea also listed. Removing blood directly lowers the red cell excess that raises blood viscosity and causes his headaches and blurry vision. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has acute promyelocytic leukemia with a translocation of chromosomes 15 and 17 and active bleeding. Which treatment approach is used for this subtype?","choices":["Tyrosine kinase inhibitor plus chemotherapy","Phlebotomy and hydroxyurea","All-trans retinoic acid plus arsenic trioxide","Hypomethylating agent plus venetoclax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute promyelocytic leukemia has a unique treatment approach: arsenic trioxide and all-trans retinoic acid. It frequently presents with disseminated intravascular coagulation and bleeding, and if the patient survives the initial presentation the cure rate is above 90%. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 40, 43, 52"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man newly diagnosed with acute myeloid leukemia asks why his team recorded an Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It rates fitness, which shapes prognosis","It stages how far the cancer has spread","It counts the blasts in his marrow","It decides his stem cell donor match"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Performance status scores are used to characterize fitness, and the prognosis of hematologic malignancies is often dictated by patient fitness, particularly in older patients like him. The score is also frequently part of clinical trial entry criteria. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with chronic myelogenous leukemia has blasts at 14% of marrow cells, dysplastic changes in granulocytes, some marrow fibrosis and significant splenomegaly. Which phase is he in?","choices":["Myelodysplastic syndrome","Accelerated phase","Chronic phase","Blast phase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Accelerated phase often has 10-19% blasts, may show dysplastic granulocytes and fibrosis, and frequently presents with significant splenomegaly; his 14% blasts with these changes fit. It is about 4-5% of presentations. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with chronic phase chronic myelogenous leukemia on a tyrosine kinase inhibitor asks whether she must take it forever. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["It stops after a fixed 6-month course","Stopping is unsafe once started","Lifelong, unless sustained deep remission","It can stop once counts are normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Treatment is for life unless the patient achieves sustained major molecular remission, at which point discontinuation is possible; recurrences occur in about half, and most who recur recapture deep responses. Most patients live full lives. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 63, 65, 66"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with myelofibrosis asks which serious complications his disease can lead to. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Kidney failure and hypercalcemia","Stroke and heart attack from platelets","Pruritus after warm showers","Portal hypertension and secondary leukemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Myelofibrosis is the most aggressive myeloproliferative neoplasm; its complications are severe splenomegaly, portal hypertension, bleeding and progression to secondary acute myeloid leukemia. High-risk patients often survive less than 2 years. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 16, 52, 53, 54"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease on home oxygen and prior heart attacks has newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia; he is unfit for aggressive therapy. Which treatment approach should be avoided?","choices":["Intensive cytarabine plus anthracycline","Hypomethylating agent plus venetoclax","Referral to a leukemia specialist","Consideration of a clinical trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intensive chemotherapy, often cytarabine plus an anthracycline, is for fit patients. He is 79 and unfit with lung and heart disease, so he should get low-intensity therapy (a hypomethylating agent with or without venetoclax) instead. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has headaches, fatigue and blurry vision, and he itches intensely after warm showers. His hemoglobin is 20.1 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) and hematocrit 61% (normal 41-50%); the spleen is enlarged. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome","Polycythemia vera"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polycythemia vera is overproduction of red cells, raising blood viscosity. It presents non-specifically with fatigue, headaches and blurry vision, the classic clue is pruritus with warm baths or showers, and splenomegaly is a complication. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 51, 52"},{"q":"A 17-year-old boy has acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and his chest X-ray shows a mediastinal mass. Which lineage is flow cytometry most likely to show?","choices":["B-cell precursor","Myeloid","Plasma cell","T-cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia is rarer, usually has lymphoma-like characteristics and may involve the thymus; a mediastinal mass may be seen on chest X-ray in T-cell disease. Flow cytometry identifies the B-cell or T-cell nature. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 19, 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with myelodysplastic syndrome asks whether any treatment can cure it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents","Myeloid growth factors","Autologous stem cell transplant","Allogeneic transplant, with high risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The only curative therapy for myelodysplastic syndrome is allogeneic stem cell transplant, but it is high risk and has a limited role in therapy. Most care is chosen by blood counts, risk status and mutations. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman is admitted with newly diagnosed acute promyelocytic leukemia. Her team warns that the first days are the most dangerous. Which complication drives her high early mortality?","choices":["Massive splenomegaly","Clotting from high platelets","Hypercalcemia and kidney injury","Bleeding from clotting failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute promyelocytic leukemia frequently presents with emergent complications, including disseminated intravascular coagulation and bleeding, and has high early mortality. If she survives the initial presentation, her prognosis is excellent. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with chronic myelogenous leukemia now has blasts at 38% of marrow cells, and the disease behaves like acute myeloid leukemia with new chromosomal abnormalities. Which phase is she in?","choices":["Chronic phase","Optimal response","Blast phase","Accelerated phase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blast phase (crisis) behaves as an acute leukemia, usually like acute myeloid leukemia, often with unique chromosomal and mutational abnormalities. Her 38% blasts are beyond the accelerated range (often 10-19%). It is 1-2% of presentations. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with newly found polycythemia vera, hypertension and a 30 pack-year smoking history asks what she should do now besides blood removal. Which advice is appropriate?","choices":["Follow up with primary care alone","Stop smoking and see a hematologist","Start oral iron supplements","Take warm baths for the itching"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with polycythemia vera should be referred to a hematologist and advised to cease smoking. Her disease already raises blood viscosity and the risk of thrombosis, and smoking adds to that risk. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"An 82-year-old man has lower-risk myelodysplastic syndrome with anemia and a very low risk of leukemia. His daughter asks what his treatment is aiming for. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Improving his quality of life","Curing the disease with transplant","Preventing imminent leukemia","Lowering his red cell mass"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lower-risk myelodysplastic syndrome has a median survival above 5 years and a very low risk of acute myeloid leukemia; these patients often have complications from anemia, bleeding or infections, and the goal of treatment is to improve quality of life. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with acute myeloid leukemia can feed herself but needs help to bathe and dress, and she spends about two-thirds of her waking hours in bed or a chair. Which Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status grade fits her?","choices":["Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4","Grade 5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She is capable of only limited self-care (she feeds herself but needs help bathing and dressing) and is confined to bed or chair more than 50% of waking hours, about two-thirds of the day. That row is grade 3. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has acute lymphoblastic leukemia, and cytogenetics show excess copies of a chromosome (hyperdiploidy). What does this finding mean for his outlook?","choices":["Poor prognosis","Adverse risk needing a transplant","Likely change to myeloid leukemia","Favorable prognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Particularly in children, acute lymphoblastic leukemia often results from excess copies of a chromosome (hyperdiploidy), which carries a favorable prognosis. Childhood disease already has five-year survival around 90%. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man has fatigue and pale skin found to be from anemia (hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL) with neutropenia. His neutrophils look abnormal, the marrow is hypercellular with dysplastic myeloid cells, and myeloid blasts are 3% of marrow cells. How should this be classified?","choices":["Myelodysplastic syndrome","Acute myeloid leukemia","Chronic myelogenous leukemia","Polycythemia vera"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myelodysplastic syndromes are defined by dysplasia (abnormal-appearing) myeloid cells, typically in a hypercellular marrow whose cells are not functional, presenting most often with anemia. Blasts at 3% are well below the 20% that would make it acute myeloid leukemia. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has fatigue, and a routine complete blood count shows unexplained anemia and thrombocytopenia with abnormal white cells. A hematologic malignancy is suspected. Which evaluation is needed to ensure the correct diagnosis?","choices":["A chemistry panel for protein levels","Chest and abdominal imaging","Bone marrow biopsy and flow cytometry","A repeat blood count in six months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hematologic malignancies frequently present with non-specific symptoms such as fatigue or are found through incidental cytopenias on a complete blood count, and almost all will require a bone marrow biopsy and/or flow cytometry to ensure the correct diagnosis. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia has a white blood cell count of 142,000 per microliter (normal 4,500-11,000). What is the next step for this white count?","choices":["Start a tyrosine kinase inhibitor","Therapeutic phlebotomy","Emergent leukapheresis","Repeat count in one week"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Presentations of acute myeloid leukemia with a white count above 200,000 are rare, and every presentation above 100,000 needs emergent leukapheresis. His count of 142,000 is above that line. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 35, 52, 63"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with myelofibrosis has massive splenomegaly causing early satiety and pain. Which treatment is often used, and with what intent?","choices":["Aspirin, preventive","Janus kinase inhibitor, palliative","Phlebotomy, curative","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor, curative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myelofibrosis, the most aggressive myeloproliferative neoplasm, is often treated with targeted Janus kinase inhibitors, which are palliative. They address the disease's Janus kinase pathway mutations without curing it. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with chronic phase chronic myelogenous leukemia has taken a tyrosine kinase inhibitor for 12 months. His BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) level is 0.4%. How should this milestone response be classified?","choices":["Failure","Treatment-free remission","Warning","Optimal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At 12 months, a BCR-ABL1 (fusion gene) level below 0.1% is optimal, 0.1-1% is a warning and above 1% is failure. His 0.4% falls in the 0.1-1% band, a warning. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with acute myeloid leukemia is in remission after initial treatment and asks what the main risk ahead is. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Progression to polycythemia vera","Iron overload from transfusion","Conversion to chronic leukemia","Relapse of the leukemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Treatment results in initial remission in many patients, but acute myeloid leukemia frequently relapses, and treatment-related mortality is high. Overall survival is only about 48% at 1 year and about 25-30% at 5 years. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 41, 52"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia has swollen, overgrown gums (gum hyperplasia) and skin nodules from leukemia outside the marrow. Which subtype group fits this picture?","choices":["Undifferentiated myeloid leukemia","Myelomonocytic or monocytic leukemia","Acute promyelocytic leukemia","Acute erythroid leukemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The acute myelomonocytic and monocytic leukemias tend to have more extramedullary involvement, and these subtypes may show gum involvement (hyperplasia). Her gum hyperplasia and skin disease outside the marrow fit them. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 33, 35"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has newly diagnosed acute lymphoblastic leukemia. He read that most children with this leukemia are cured and asks whether his outlook is the same. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Outlook depends on his blood type","Outlook is poorer and falls with age","Outlook matches that of children","Outlook is better than in children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The outlook for acute lymphoblastic leukemia is often positive particularly for children, but outcomes for adults over 40 are poor, and the cure rate decreases by age. Results are improving with new treatment options, including immunotherapies. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 28"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with essential thrombocythemia and platelets of 1,600,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000) has gum bleeding and easy bruising. Which explains bleeding despite his high platelets?","choices":["Hyperviscosity from red cells","Acquired von Willebrand factor shortage","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Marrow failure lowering platelets"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In essential thrombocythemia, bleeding with high platelets can occur from acquired von Willebrand factor deficiency, leaving a shortage of factor at the site of injury. Clotting remains its main complication. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 15-year-old boy has newly diagnosed acute lymphoblastic leukemia. Before treatment, his team wants to know whether the leukemia involves the central nervous system, an adverse risk factor. Which procedure answers this?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Bone marrow biopsy","Flow cytometry of blood","Chest X-ray"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central nervous system involvement can occur in acute lymphoblastic leukemia and is an adverse risk factor, so lumbar puncture is an important part of the work-up alongside blood tests and imaging. — 49. Hematologic Malignancies Part I - Gannon.pptx, Slide 25"}]};
